; Study 12 — a behaviour-only trait as the target's interface
;
; The Rust I want:
;   trait Named    -- with one method, `label`, returning a String
;   impl Named for User, impl Named for Bot
;   fn show(n: &dyn Named) -> String
;
; A Ranger `trait` is a MIXIN: its methods are copied into each class that
; `does` it. That is the right lowering for a trait carrying FIELDS — no
; target has anywhere to put them, since each consumer already owns its own
; copy — but a trait that declares only BEHAVIOUR is exactly what an interface
; says, and every statically typed target has one:
;
;   Rust    a `trait`, an `impl` per consumer, `Rc<RefCell<dyn …>>` as a type
;   C++     an abstract base class with a pure virtual, and public inheritance
;   Go      an interface — structural, so nothing has to declare it conforms
;   Java    an `interface` in a file of its own, and `implements`
;   Kotlin  an `interface`, and the copied bodies become `override`s of it
;   C#      an `interface`
;   Dart    an abstract class, and `implements`
;   Swift   a `protocol`, and a conformance
;
; Using one as a TYPE used to be silent broken output on every one of those:
; the writer emitted the trait's NAME and declared nothing of that name, so
; Ranger reported success and the target compiler stopped at "cannot find
; type Named" or its local equivalent. The field-bearing case is refused
; instead — see `attempts/04_trait_as_type.rgr` under rust/ and cpp/.
;
; `Sized2` below is deliberate: nothing names it as a type, so it stays a pure
; mixin. Giving an interface to every class that consumes any behaviour-only
; trait is a layout change — a vtable, an itable — for programs that never
; asked for one.

trait Named {
    fn label:string () {
        return "anon"
    }
}

trait Sized2 {
    fn weight:int () {
        return 1
    }
}

class User {
    does Named
    does Sized2
    def uname:string ""
}

class Bot {
    does Named
    def id:int 0
}

class TraitsMain {
    fn show:string (n:Named) {
        return (n.label())
    }

    sfn main:void () {
        def app:TraitsMain (new TraitsMain())
        def u:User (new User())
        def b:Bot (new Bot())
        print ("user " + (app.show(u)))
        print ("bot " + (app.show(b)))
        print ("weight " + (to_string (u.weight())))
    }
}
